    {
      "id": "v858ayb",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning and Characteristics of Ocean Currents"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Ocean currents are large-scale continuous movements of ocean water driven by wind, gravity, temperature, and density gradients.",
        "Ocean gyres form large circular current patterns in the Atlantic, Pacific, and Indian Oceans influenced by the Coriolis effect.",
        "Thermohaline circulation operates as a deep-ocean global conveyor belt transferring heat and nutrients worldwide.",
        "Upwelling brings cold, nutrient-rich deep water to the surface, supporting rich marine life and major fishing zones."
      ],
      "id": "6uu3vbd"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fgghfiv",
      "content": "• Ocean currents are large-scale horizontal flows of ocean water."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "12izggy",
      "content": "• They behave like rivers within the ocean."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gb9afoh",
      "content": "• Speed is generally higher at the surface and slower in deep waters."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "o09sp87",
      "content": "• Direction is influenced by Coriolis force.<img></img>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wlrpdks",
      "content": "• Currents are deflected by continental margins creating circular patterns called gyres."
    },
    {
      "id": "63gp2qx",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Warm vs Cold Ocean Currents"
    },
